## 3. The five most important end-to-end workflows

These workflows are the heart of Godavari One. Build them before adding secondary features.

### Workflow A — Emergency SOS to verified resolution

1. Pilgrim submits SOS

   Selects emergency type, shares location and provides optional details.
2. Backend creates incident

   Assigns unique ID, timestamp, location, severity and status.
3. Command centre receives alert

   Shows the incident on the map and in the active incident queue.
4. Operator assigns responder

   Selects an available authorized team and records the assignment.
5. Responder accepts and navigates

   Receives incident details and approved location information.
6. Responder updates progress

   En route → arrived → assisting → resolved or escalated.
7. Operator verifies closure

   Records the outcome and closes the case with an audit trail.

### Workflow B — Crowd buildup to approved intervention

1. Receive camera-derived counts or simulated footfall data.
2. Calculate density estimates and rate of change for a mapped zone.
3. Trigger a configurable threshold alert.
4. Show the hotspot, confidence, source and time on the command map.
5. Operator reviews the alert and approves or rejects an intervention.
6. Authorized teams receive tasks; an approved public alert is sent if required.
7. System records actions and tracks the hotspot until it is cleared.

### Workflow C — Missing person to verified reunification

1. Pilgrim or help-desk operator creates a missing-person case.
2. System generates a private case ID and captures only necessary information.
3. Authorized help desks and staff can search relevant open cases.
4. A possible match is raised for human review, not automatically confirmed.
5. Authorized staff verify identity and guardian relationship.
6. Reunification is recorded, with appropriate confirmation and audit history.
7. The case is closed and access to sensitive information is restricted.

### Workflow D — Sanitation report to completed task

1. User scans the facility QR code or chooses a facility on the map.
2. Reports a problem such as unclean toilet, water shortage or damage.
3. System creates a maintenance ticket.
4. Supervisor assigns a field worker.
5. Worker marks work started and submits completion evidence.
6. Supervisor verifies completion.
7. Facility status and service history update.

### Workflow E — Offline incident to synchronized case

1. Responder or field worker loses connectivity.
2. App stores permitted updates in a local encrypted queue.
3. User can see which actions are pending synchronization.
4. When the connection returns, the client submits queued events with unique IDs.
5. Backend deduplicates repeated submissions and applies valid updates.
6. Conflicts are flagged for review rather than silently overwriting records.

## 4. Technical architecture



Recommended architecture

&#x20;Modular monolith first, service separation later.



As a solo developer, avoid starting with 10 microservices, Kubernetes and complicated distributed infrastructure. Build one well-structured backend with independent modules, clear interfaces and background workers. It can be separated into services when traffic and operational needs justify it.

