### 4.4 Database design

Use PostgreSQL as the source of truth. PostGIS should handle locations, zones, routes and proximity queries.

Core tables:

| Table                 | Important fields                                       | Purpose                          |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
| `users`               | id, name, phone/email, status                          | User accounts                    |
| `roles`               | id, role_name                                          | Permission groups                |
| `user_roles`          | user_id, role_id, department_id                        | Role assignment                  |
| `departments`         | id, name, contact                                      | Police, medical, municipal, etc. |
| `zones`               | id, name, boundary geometry, risk settings             | Ghats and operational areas      |
| `facilities`          | id, name, type, location, status                       | Toilets, hospitals, help desks   |
| `incidents`           | id, type, severity, status, location, created_at       | Main incident record             |
| `incident_events`     | id, incident_id, event_type, actor_id, timestamp       | Immutable event history          |
| `assignments`         | incident_id, responder_id, assigned_at, status         | Dispatch records                 |
| `responder_locations` | responder_id, location, recorded_at                    | Authorized team locations        |
| `lost_person_cases`   | id, case_type, restricted_details, status              | Missing/found cases              |
| `crowd_observations`  | zone_id, count_estimate, confidence, source, timestamp | Crowd data                       |
| `alerts`              | id, message, language, target_zone, approval_status    | Public announcements             |
| `field_tasks`         | id, facility_id, assigned_to, status, due_at           | Field work                       |
| `task_evidence`       | task_id, file_id, submitted_by, timestamp              | Completion evidence              |
| `sync_operations`     | client_operation_id, user_id, status                   | Offline synchronization          |
| `audit_logs`          | actor_id, action, resource, timestamp                  | Security and accountability      |

Use UUIDs for primary identifiers. Use geometry/geography columns for mapped data, spatial indexes for location searches, and UTC timestamps internally. Display event-local time in the interface.

Data design rules

- Store operational changes as events, not just overwrites of a status field.
- Keep sensitive missing-person information in restricted tables or fields with stricter access.
- Separate public facility information from internal operational notes.
- Store large media in object storage; store references and metadata in PostgreSQL.
- Define retention policies for location history, SOS records, photos and personal information before a real deployment.

