## 9. Security, privacy and reliability requirements

This is not an ordinary event-management application. It may handle sensitive personal information and safety-related decisions.

### Minimum security controls

| Area                  | Required control                                              |
| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Authentication        | Strong authentication for staff and administrators            |
| Authorization         | RBAC plus department and geographic scope                     |
| Data in transit       | HTTPS/TLS                                                     |
| Data at rest          | Encrypted database and object storage                         |
| Sensitive information | Restricted access and minimized collection                    |
| Lost-person reports   | Private-by-default access and controlled disclosure           |
| Audit                 | Append-only records of sensitive actions                      |
| File uploads          | Type validation, size limits and malware scanning             |
| API                   | Rate limiting, input validation and secure session management |
| Backups               | Automated backups and tested restoration                      |
| Public alerts         | Draft, approval and publication history                       |
| Location tracking     | Authorized purpose, restricted access and retention limits    |

Before a real deployment, have the responsible government and security teams review the applicable Indian data-protection, cybersecurity, emergency-response and procurement requirements.

### Reliability scenarios to test

1. Internet connection disappears during SOS submission.
2. Same SOS is submitted multiple times.
3. Two operators assign the same responder.
4. Responder phone restarts with unsynchronized updates.
5. GPS is unavailable or inaccurate.
6. Camera feed stops sending observations.
7. Crowd model returns a low-confidence estimate.
8. An unauthorized user attempts to access a missing-person report.
9. An alert is drafted but not approved.
10. Database or notification provider becomes unavailable.

For each scenario, define the expected user experience and recovery procedure. A reliable system must make uncertainty visible rather than displaying false certainty.

## 10. Testing and performance measurement

Your product should demonstrate measurable performance, not just attractive UI screens.

| Metric                    | What to measure                                            |
| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| SOS acknowledgement time  | Submission to command-centre acknowledgement               |
| Dispatch time             | Acknowledgement to responder assignment                    |
| Responder acceptance time | Assignment to acceptance                                   |
| Incident closure time     | Submission to verified resolution                          |
| Duplicate handling        | Percentage of duplicate cases correctly flagged for review |
| Offline synchronization   | Successful queued operations after reconnection            |
| Crowd model quality       | Counting error on a labelled test dataset                  |
| Alert latency             | Observation to operator-visible alert                      |
| Notification delivery     | Successful versus failed delivery                          |
| Field task completion     | Assigned, completed and supervisor-verified tasks          |
| System availability       | Service uptime and recovery time                           |

Use test data and repeatable simulations to establish baselines. Do not claim actual emergency response improvements until the system has been evaluated in a controlled pilot with authorized personnel.

### Suggested acceptance criteria for your own prototype

These are proposed engineering targets, not official challenge requirements:

- All critical incident transitions are recorded.
- Duplicate client submissions do not create duplicate incidents.
- Unauthorized roles cannot access restricted records.
- Offline operations synchronize without silently losing history.
- Dashboard updates are visible within a defined target latency under your test load.
- Crowd observations display source, timestamp and confidence.
- Every public safety announcement requires an authorized approval step.
- Every major workflow has automated tests.

